    "text": "To solve the equation \\((2^x - 2)(5 \\cdot 2^x + 10) = 3 \\cdot 4^x + 12\\), we will proceed step by step.\n\nFirst, let's rewrite \\(4^x\\) as \\((2^2)^x = (2^x)^2\\). The equation then becomes:\n\\[\n(2^x - 2)(5 \\cdot 2^x + 10) = 3(2^x)^2 + 12\n\\]\n\nNext, let's expand the left-hand side of the equation:\n\\[\n(2^x - 2)(5 \\cdot 2^x + 10) = 2^x \\cdot 5 \\cdot 2^x + 2^x \\cdot 10 - 2 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 2^x - 2 \\cdot 10\n\\]\n\\[\n= 5 \\cdot (2^x)^2 + 10 \\cdot 2^x - 10 \\cdot 2^x - 20\n\\]\n\\[\n= 5 \\cdot (2^x)^2 - 20\n\\]\n\nNow, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the equation:\n\\[\n3(2^x)^2 + 12\n\\]\n\nSo the equation now is:\n\\[\n5 \\cdot (2^x)^2 - 20 = 3 \\cdot (2^x)^2 + 12\n\\]\n\nNext, let's isolate the terms involving \\((2^x)^2\\) on one side of the equation:\n\\[\n5 \\cdot (2^x)^2 - 3 \\cdot (2^x)^2 = 12 + 20\n\\]\n\\[\n2 \\cdot (2^x)^2 = 32\n\\]\n\nNow, solve for \\((2^x)^2\\):\n\\[\n(2^x)^2 = \\frac{32}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\n(2^x)^2 = 16\n\\]\n\nTaking the square root of both sides, we get:\n\\[\n2^x = 4 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad 2^x = -4\n\\]\n\nSince \\(2^x\\) is always positive for real \\(x\\), we discard \\(2^x = -4\\). Therefore:\n\\[\n2^x = 4\n\\]\n\nWe know that \\(4 = 2^2\\), so:\n\\[\n2^x = 2^2\n\\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[\nx = 2\n\\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]",
    "timing_scope": "native prompt render, tokenize, transfer, CUDA-synchronized generate, decode; excludes loading/scoring",
